These three Articles are highly reformative Articles when compared with the provisions in the Criminal Code, which in Articles 281 to 296 regulate provisions, where the heaviest criminal sanction is Article 295 which is 12 years. When compared, the Child Protection Law has been quite severe in establishing criminal and fine sanctions as regulated in Articles 81 to 83.

The problem is, even though the Law has established severe sanctions or punishments, there are still Law Enforcement Officials who have not optimally applied them, so there is still a long way to go, between law in book and law in action. In cases as regulated in Articles 81 to 83, Law Officials often conduct investigations and inquiries that are quite heavy and exhausting for child victims of violence, but in prosecutions and judges' verdicts are still light so they do not accord with justice.

Violence against women and children also occurs frequently within the household sphere, even though we have had a law for 13 years, namely Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (KDRT), but nowadays domestic violence is increasing in number. For the prevention and handling of domestic violence in Musi Rawas Regency, the Office of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of Musi Rawas Regency has established the Integrated Service Center for the Protection of Women and Children (P2TP2A) of Musi Rawas Regency, based on the Decree of the Regent of Musi Rawas Number 703/KPTS/KPP/2015 concerning the Establishment of the Integrated Service Center for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (P2TP2A) of Musi Rawas Regency. The Integrated Service Center for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (P2TP2A) serves as a place for reporting, handling, rehabilitation and reintegration of victims of violence. If there is domestic violence or other violence affecting women and children, it must be reported to the P2TP2A, however the existence of P2TP2A is not yet widely known by the community.

Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, essentially aims to realize a household that is sakinah, mawadah, and warohmah, this can be read from the objectives of eliminating domestic violence namely: (a) preventing all forms of domestic violence (KDRT); (b) protecting victims of domestic violence, (c) taking action against perpetrators of domestic violence and (d) maintaining the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household. So that we better understand the prohibition of domestic violence, the important matters of Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Domestic Violence will be briefly described as follows:

Types of domestic violence, include:

a. physical violence, is an act that results in (1) pain, (2) illness, or (3) serious injury;
b. psychological violence, is an act that results in (1) fear, (2) loss of self-confidence, (3) loss of ability to act, (4) feeling of powerlessness, (5) and/or severe psychological suffering to a person;
c. sexual violence includes: (1) forced sexual relations committed against a person including a child, residing within the household sphere, (2) forced sexual relations against someone within the household sphere with another person including a child, for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes;
d. household neglect, includes (1) neglecting a person including a child within the household sphere, whereas according to the law applicable to them or because of an agreement or arrangement they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person or child, (2) causing economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

The rights of domestic violence victims are to receive:

(1) protection from family, police, prosecution, court, advocate, social institution, or other parties either temporarily or based on a court order;

(2) health services according to medical needs; (3) special handling related to victim confidentiality;

(4) accompaniment by social workers and legal assistance at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations and

(5) spiritual guidance services. All parties both from government elements, Law Enforcement Officials (APH) and NGOs, have become members of the P2TP2A of Musi Rawas Regency.

